# README Updates

Every code change should be evaluated for whether [README.md](README.md) needs updating. When the change affects something README currently documents (or should document), update README in the same change.

## Update README When a Change

- Adds, removes, or changes a user-facing feature, screen, control, or behavior
- Changes supported platforms, BLE behavior, operating modes, or architecture components listed in README
- Adds, removes, or renames modules/files under the documented project structure
- Changes the technology stack, dependencies, or how to build/run the app (once those sections exist)

## Skip README Updates When a Change

- Is internal-only (refactors, test-only, DI wiring) with no effect on features, structure, or usage described in README
- Fixes a bug that restores already-documented intended behavior
- Touches files or concerns README does not cover and does not need to cover yet

## Guidelines

- Match README's existing sections and tone (overview tables, architecture table, project structure tree).
- Update only the sections affected; avoid unrelated rewrites.
- Do not document features in README until they are ready for users.
- Detailed design belongs in [documentation/SDD.md](documentation/SDD.md); README stays concise and user/developer oriented.
- If README does not need an update for a functional change, the final summary must explicitly state that README was considered and why it was not changed.
